00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.150
Leah: So why is it important to carry out coding reviews?

00:00:03.700 --> 00:00:09.850
As with any form of writing, coding involves the
use of language and just as with a writer, poet, or

00:00:09.850 --> 00:00:13.339
dramatist the coders work needs to be ‘proofread’.

00:00:14.219 --> 00:00:20.419
When writing code, it is good practice to have someone
checking the work that you’re doing as even the most

00:00:20.419 --> 00:00:23.929
experienced developers can make mistakes or miss something.

00:00:24.599 --> 00:00:30.579
Chris: Reviews also significantly ‘improve
code quality’, ‘save time’, and can help

00:00:30.580 --> 00:00:32.450
‘streamline’ the development process.

00:00:33.070 --> 00:00:39.870
The results of reviewing can give opportunities for ‘better
design’, and suggest more appropriate solutions, or more

00:00:40.130 --> 00:00:43.370
‘effective ways of working’, within the project framework.

00:00:44.370 --> 00:00:49.210
Leah: Code reviews also help with ‘knowledge
transfer’ from more experienced developers to

00:00:49.660 --> 00:00:55.379
those with less experience, enabling them to learn
more ‘reliable techniques’ and ‘best practice’.

00:00:56.470 --> 00:01:01.160
It also helps with the ‘creation of better
documentation’ which will be useful when

00:01:01.170 --> 00:01:03.440
new features or upgrades may be needed.

00:01:04.210 --> 00:01:07.219
Chris: Reviewing makes ‘Quality assurance testing easier’.

00:01:08.009 --> 00:01:12.865
If there are consistent standards in the source
code, it makes it easier for specialists or QA

00:01:13.370 --> 00:01:18.880
testers to understand, which will in turn minimise
the need for further testing and reworking.

00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:24.340
Leah: Code reviews in ‘Agile’ follow the more
streamlined approaches such as peer review and

00:01:24.340 --> 00:01:26.960
peer programming which are less disruptive.

00:01:27.630 --> 00:01:33.239
Peer reviews are a defining principle of Agile,
playing a critical role in creating consistency

00:01:33.510 --> 00:01:39.050
in the source code, and setting the stage for
better ‘QA testing’ and smoother integration.

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:47.609
An important part or the reviewing process is giving
feedback this should always be constructive and effective.

00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:52.140
Chris: Effective feedback asks
questions instead of demanding changes.

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:58.750
It gives guidance based on common rules, not just
personal opinion and should always ‘add value’.

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:06.700
The way you comment on the code is as important as what you
are saying, so being kind and respectful goes a long way.

00:02:07.290 --> 00:02:12.260
Leah: Constructive feedback should be specific
and give examples based on documentation

00:02:12.700 --> 00:02:14.810
and explain reasons for any comments.

00:02:15.380 --> 00:02:20.579
It is best to avoid repeating comments for
the same error and ask for clarification

00:02:20.599 --> 00:02:22.670
if a segment of code isn’t understood.

00:02:23.499 --> 00:02:29.000
It is also important to address the team using
collective language using ‘we’ instead of ‘you’.

00:02:29.980 --> 00:02:34.999
‘Effective feedback’ can reward developers
by including positive comments and expressing

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:37.179
appreciation for well written code.

00:02:38.089 --> 00:02:42.200
Chris: ‘Effective feedback’ will help to
improve the quality of code and can help

00:02:42.200 --> 00:02:44.519
find bugs before the code reaches production.

00:02:45.049 --> 00:02:51.579
It will also ‘promote sharing knowledge’, increase
confidence and trust, align teams, and raise the

00:02:51.579 --> 00:02:54.369
standard of communication and work within the team.

00:02:55.230 --> 00:03:00.149
More ‘effective communication’ and ‘improved
collaboration’ in the team will help make them more

00:03:00.150 --> 00:03:03.720
engaged due to a higher level of confidence and trust.

